INT. BOARDING HOUSE - KITCHEN - MORNING - 1911

A long, low room with a range stove and a scrubbed deal table. Copper pans hang from a rack. Rain on the window. A bowl of apples. A kettle sings on the hob.

ABIGAIL THORNBURY (50s, widow, apron over grey wool, hands red from lye) rolls pastry. She does it without thinking, as her mother did, and her mother's mother: out, turn, out, turn.

Through the ceiling comes the sound of a bed creaking. A step. A pause. A drawer slides shut.

ROSALIE (O.S.)
(from upstairs, muffled)
Abigail? Are you there?

ABIGAIL
(raising her voice slightly)
Where else would I be?

ROSALIE (O.S.)
I can't find my blue hat.

ABIGAIL
It's on the hook in the hall.

ROSALIE (O.S.)
I looked there.

ABIGAIL
Look again.

A pause. A longer creak. Then footsteps, light, down the back stairs. The kitchen door swings open and ROSALIE THORNBURY-LAMB (50s, younger sister, travelling coat, hat in hand) enters, pink in the cheeks.

ROSALIE
It was on the hook.

ABIGAIL
It's always on the hook.

ROSALIE
I don't know how I missed it.

ABIGAIL
You were looking for a hat. You weren't looking for a hook.

Rosalie sets the hat down on the table, beside the apples. She doesn't sit. Abigail rolls and turns the pastry.

ROSALIE
Is there tea?

ABIGAIL
The kettle's on.

Rosalie takes two cups from the dresser. Arranges them, saucers, spoons. Pours when the kettle shrieks. She sets Abigail's cup on the table at her elbow and takes her own to the window.

ROSALIE
It's still raining.

ABIGAIL
It's been raining since Michaelmas.

ROSALIE
She'd have hated that. Mother. Always said rain made the apples sour.

ABIGAIL
She said many things.

ROSALIE
She said you'd stay.

Abigail's hands pause on the pin. Only for a second. Then they roll.

ABIGAIL
And she said you'd go. Both are true.

ROSALIE
Are they?

ABIGAIL
Nobody's stopping you.

ROSALIE
I thought you might.

ABIGAIL
I'd be a poor sister to bar a door.

Rosalie sips her tea. Her face is turned to the window and the grey light; Abigail's is turned to the table. Neither sees the other's expression.

ROSALIE
The train is at noon.

ABIGAIL
Then you'll want an early lunch.

ROSALIE
I will.

ABIGAIL
I've an apple pie.

ROSALIE
It's not baked.

ABIGAIL
It will be.

She lifts a flat circle of pastry and drapes it over a tin. Trims the edge with a knife in one smooth turn. Rosalie watches the knife.

ROSALIE
You've always been so good at that.

ABIGAIL
It's the only thing I'm good at.

ROSALIE
That isn't true.

ABIGAIL
(drily)
Tell me what else, then.

ROSALIE
You were good at being left.

Abigail's knife stops. She doesn't look up.

ABIGAIL
That's unkind.

ROSALIE
It's the truth. I mean it kindly.

ABIGAIL
I know you do.

A long, quiet moment. The kettle ticks as it cools. Rosalie crosses, sets down her cup. Takes an apple from the bowl, then a second, and hands one to Abigail. They peel in silence, side by side, the skins coming off in two unbroken spirals.

ROSALIE
Yours is longer.

ABIGAIL
It always is.

She drops her spiral in the bin. Rosalie keeps hers, curled in her palm like a small snake, a moment longer than she needs to.

ROSALIE
I'll write.

ABIGAIL
You won't.

ROSALIE
I will.

ABIGAIL
Then I'll read it.

Rosalie smiles. She sets the peel on the table. It uncurls slowly and lies there, pale, in the lamplight.

FADE OUT.
